WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.410
The paper carefully ablates where stochastic
anchoring should be applied.

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:08.161
On the D and D dataset, which has the most severe
size shift,

00:00:08.241 --> 00:00:15.419
applying anchoring after the readout layer
dramatically improves both accuracy and calibration as network depth grows,

00:00:15.499 --> 00:00:18.494
while earlier-layer anchoring converges less well.

00:00:18.574 --> 00:00:24.214
This leads to a practical recommendation to anchor
at the last layer for size-shift settings.

00:00:24.294 --> 00:00:27.474
The authors also validate that partial stochasticity,

00:00:27.554 --> 00:00:32.578
in the form of the pretrained variant that only
trains an anchored classifier head,

00:00:32.658 --> 00:00:34.485
is both effective and scalable,

00:00:34.565 --> 00:00:39.713
and a neural tangent kernel analysis confirms that
intermediate constant shifts truly

00:00:39.793 --> 00:00:44.510
alter the model's function rather than acting as a
trivial reparameterization.
